# Let the stack release the head camera without a human at a terminal.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `/dev/video4` is single-owner and Unitree's `videohub_pc4`
# holds it from boot. Freeing it for the detector means stopping master_service
# and reaping the children it leaves behind — both root actions. Without this,
# `take_camera` can only run where sudo can prompt, so any unattended bring-up
# (`perception_up nav2` over ssh, a systemd unit, a scripted demo) ends with
# "the detector will stay offline" and a note telling somebody to go and type it
# by hand. That is not a safety property, it is a missing permission dressed as
# one.
#
# Priming the sudo timestamp first does NOT work here: this build keys the
# timestamp to the parent pid, so a grandchild — which is exactly what
# take_camera is when perception_up runs it — gets a fresh, unprimed check.
#
# NARROW ON PURPOSE. Two exact commands, no wildcards in the program path, no
# shell. It cannot start anything, cannot touch the motion API, and cannot be
# used to stop an arbitrary unit: `systemctl stop` is pinned to the one unit
# name. Everything that can actually move the robot still goes through the
# bridge and its closed gate.
#
# The reverse — `systemctl start master_service` — is deliberately NOT here.
# Giving the camera back is not urgent, is not scripted, and a human doing it
# deliberately is the right shape for the one direction that takes a sensor away
# from the other team's running app.
unitree ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop master_service
unitree ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pkill -9 -f videohub_pc4
